### 1. Concept Deep-Dive
An interface specifies a contract of abstract capabilities without maintaining instance state.
* **Interface Rules**:
  * All fields in an interface are implicitly `public static final` constants.
  * Abstract methods have no body and end with a semicolon.
* **Default Methods (`default`)**:
  * Introduced in Java 8 to allow interfaces to provide default implementation logic.
  * Classes implementing the interface inherit the default method automatically unless they choose to `@Override` it.
* **Multiple Inheritance of Type**: A class can implement multiple interfaces (`implements A, B`), solving the single inheritance constraint of classes.

### 2. Interactive Code Walkthrough
```java
interface Measurable {
    double getMetricValue(); // Abstract method

    // Default method providing ready-to-use behavior
    default boolean isWithinTolerance(double target, double tolerance) {
        return Math.abs(getMetricValue() - target) <= tolerance;
    }
}

class PressureValve implements Measurable {
    private double psi;
    public PressureValve(double psi) { this.psi = psi; }

    @Override
    public double getMetricValue() { return psi; }
}
```

