### 1. Concept Deep-Dive
When handling objects through generic parent references, runtime type inspection allows identifying specific subclass capabilities.
* **The `instanceof` Operator**: Evaluates whether an object reference is an instance of a given class or interface: `if (obj instanceof SubClass)`. Evaluates to `false` if `obj` is `null`.
* **Explicit Downcasting**: Casting a parent reference down to a subclass: `SubClass s = (SubClass) obj;`.
  * *Critical Hazard*: If `obj` is not an instance of `SubClass`, the JVM throws a fatal `ClassCastException`. Always guard with `instanceof`.
* **Pattern Matching for `instanceof`**: Modern Java syntax `if (obj instanceof SubClass s)` automatically binds and casts `s`.

### 2. Interactive Code Walkthrough
```java
public class TypeInspectionDemo {
    public static void inspect(Vehicle v) {
        if (v == null) {
            System.out.println("No vehicle");
            return;
        }

        if (v instanceof ElectricVehicle) {
            ElectricVehicle ev = (ElectricVehicle) v; // Safe downcast
            System.out.println("Electric vehicle detected!");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Standard combustion vehicle");
        }
    }
}
```

